│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ shared_crz_ring_regularized                                                               │
│ patch_description        │ The current best-scoring candidate (score 0.85) uses 8 independent CRZ parameters in its  │
│                          │ entangling ring, leading to high train accuracy (0.98) but a large generalization gap     │
│                          │ (0.12) due to overfitting. The second prior program, which shared CRZ parameters pairwise │
│                          │ (crz_a/crz_b alternating) achieved a better combined score (0.86) with fewer parameters   │
│                          │ (110) and lower gap (0.07).                                                               │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I apply the same parameter-sharing principle to the current ring topology: instead of 8   │
│                          │ independent CRZ angles, use only 2 shared parameters alternating around the ring (crz_a,  │
│                          │ crz_b, crz_a, crz_b, ...). This halves the number of entangler parameters per block (from │
│                          │ 8 to 2), significantly reducing total parameter count and regularizing against            │
│                          │ overfitting, while preserving full ring connectivity for information mixing across        │
│                          │ upload/repeat blocks. This follows the recommendation to keep the topology's global       │
│                          │ correlation potential while reducing redundant free parameters that likely just memorize  │
│                          │ training noise.                                                                           │
