│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ none                                                                                      │
│ patch_description        │ Builds on the winning design's insight that aggressive parameter sharing (grouping qubits │
│                          │ into pairs with shared RY/RZ angles) improves generalization and convergence speed. The   │
│                          │ novel contribution here is a **dual-ring entangling topology**: instead of a single       │
│                          │ nearest-neighbor CRZ ring, we alternate nearest-neighbor (distance-1) and                 │
│                          │ next-nearest-neighbor (distance-2) CRZ couplings around the ring, each sharing just two   │
│                          │ parameters (crz_a, crz_b) across their respective ring positions. This creates richer,    │
│                          │ longer-range entanglement (distance-1 AND distance-2 connectivity) using the same tiny    │
│                          │ parameter count as the seed's single ring, since distance-2 connections let information   │
│                          │ mix across more distant qubits within one block — reducing the number of blocks/uploads   │
│                          │ needed to spread correlations across all 8 qubits. We keep the qubit-pair-grouped RY/RZ   │
│                          │ pre-rotation layer (4 shared angles each) that proved effective for both accuracy and     │
│                          │ generalization gap in the prior program, and retain the shared post-rotation RZ layer for │
│                          │ phase realignment before the next feature upload. This keeps the parameter count          │
│                          │ essentially the same (88 vs 86) while giving the entangler more topological               │
│                          │ expressiveness per parameter, which should improve convergence speed and possibly closes  │
│                          │ the generalization gap further by giving the circuit more expressive, though still highly │
│                          │ constrained (shared), interactions.                                                       │
│ num_applied              │ 1                                                                                         │
│ api_costs                │ $0.1442                                                                                   │
